# Anti-patterns — what NOT to do

These are real failures pulled from generated UI Angel rejected. If your output resembles any of these, it's wrong.

## 1. The divider-heavy list (the #1 generic-AI tell)
Stacking list items with a `border-bottom` or `divide-y` line between each. Looks like a settings menu from 2014.
- **Instead:** spacing rhythm → tonal surface per item → highlight block for the active row only.

## 2. The letterboxed media blob (RuForge Music album row)
A square album cover crammed into the wrong aspect ratio, padded with black side-bars, sitting on a black card. It reads as a muddy dark smear. Worst offender.
- **Instead:** `object-fit: cover`, crop to fill the frame, round the corners. Art fills its container edge to edge. If you must show the whole non-cropped image, the *frame* matches the art's aspect ratio. Never bars.

## 3. Everything-looks-the-same sections
"Albums", "Recently added", "Rediscover" rendered as three pixel-identical rows of identical cards. Tells the user nothing about what matters.
- **Instead:** sameness signals equal value. Differentiate sections that carry different weight by card size, art treatment, or density.

## 4. Decorative glow
Neon edges, colored box-shadow halos, "make it pop" outer glow. Reads cheap and dated.
- **Instead:** depth from tonal layers. If something needs to lift, a soft functional shadow. Never a glow.

## 5. Shadow on every card by default
Drop shadows sprayed on every surface "for depth." Flattens into noise and signals nothing.
- **Instead:** tonal contrast for normal depth. Shadow ONLY to signal floating media or scroll-state.

## 6. The flat low-contrast dark card (the current Cursor default)
A single dark card on a barely-different dark background, faint text, no layering, dividers inside. No tonal hierarchy, nothing floats, nothing reads as primary.
- **Instead:** a real tonal ramp (surface-0 → surface-3), characterful type, weight/color hierarchy, spacing-based separation.

## 7. Inter on a flat white card
The default generated look: system-ui or Inter, pure `#fff` card, pure `#000` text, hard divider lines.
- **Instead:** characterful sans (Satoshi/General Sans), a tonal hue base (not pure white), weight + muted-color hierarchy.

## 8. Corporate empty-state cheer
"Oops! Looks like there's nothing here yet! 🎉🎉" on a screen that should have voice, OR a jokey tone on a settings screen that should be precise.
- **Instead:** voice by surface. Casual-human on empty states ("dang.. nothing here yet"), plain-precise on functional screens.

## 9. Reinvented info placement
Putting the duration timestamp top-left, the rating in a weird spot, rank badges floating randomly. Forces the user to re-learn the layout.
- **Instead:** learned conventions. Duration bottom-right, rank top-left, metadata in a dot-separated line under the card.

## 10. Animating layout properties
Transitioning `width`/`height`/`top`/`left`/`margin` → janky, laggy, drops frames.
- **Instead:** `transform` and `opacity` only.

## 11. Hard truncate with no hover reveal
Device names, track titles, paths, or menu labels cut off with `…` and no way to read the rest on hover. Hides meaning.
- **Instead:** truncate at rest; slow marquee on hover / row hover (`HoverMarqueeText`, `animate-marquee-slow` for menus).

## 12. The accent-bar section label (vertical red slit)
A 2–4px rounded accent bar beside every section title (`| All-time top tracks`). Reads like generic generated UI.
- **Instead:** typographic hierarchy only: size, weight, tracking, and spacing. Match Music Home section heads (`text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight`). Separate sections with gap or tonal panels, not decorator bars.

## 13. Engraved / stamped controls
Chips, buttons, badges, or media frames with dual-lip inset shadows (light top edge + dark bottom edge) or a deep carved sink (`inset 2px 2px 8px…`) so the control looks pressed into the panel.
- **Instead:** tonal fills only for resting controls. Soft outer shadow only when something floats (menus, dialogs). Large well vignettes on page shells stay allowed — that is not engraving a button.

## 14. Indicator dots
Small colored circles used as status, unread, priority, or category markers (in tooltips, rows, badges, tabs, anywhere). Banned everywhere, app and website.
- **Instead:** say it in text, or use the element's own icon or color. Dot-separated metadata text (`·`) is fine; a drawn dot is not.

## 15. Fake "chips"
A text capsule (pill) or an icon in a padded tile is not a chip. When Angel asks for a chip on a card, he means a shape docked into a card corner that fits tight to the edges: a notched corner tab with concave fillet curves where it meets the card (reference: `RoadmapReadySection.tsx`, `.rf-roadmap-ready-notch`).
- **Instead:** cut the corner with a notch in the background color, fillet both joins with radial-gradient curves, and seat the chip in the notch. Default to the top-right corner and float it, so the text starts at the left edge, runs up to the chip, and the next line wraps under it (the eye barely moves). Size the card to its content. No big empty zones reserved for the chip.

## 16. Glassy / translucent cards
Cards filled with low-alpha tints (`rgb(... / 0.06)`) over a tinted panel read fuzzy and glassy. So does a panel wrapping a card grid (an inner well).
- **Instead:** solid opaque tonal fills, and cards sitting straight on the page background.
